  "installNotes": [
   "15kW appears only via Honeywell (G0062610) and Centurion (G0062810) SKUs sharing the 0J9943/0J9944 manual generation - no dedicated 15kW spec column found; treated as a GT-990 variant between 14 and 16kW",
   "Clearance: 3 ft (0.91 m) ends/front, 18 in (457 mm) back, 5 ft (1.52 m) top and from operable windows/doors (0K5804)",
   "Required fuel pressure at regulator: 3.5-7 in WC (9-13 mm Hg) NG, 10-12 in WC (19-22 mm Hg) LP (0K5804 p.23 and connection diagram callout)",
   "Ships on composite pad; concrete pad optional per local code",
   "Battery charger integrated into control panel (Smart Charger)"
  ],
  "tips": [
   "15 kW appears only as Honeywell (G0062610) and Centurion (G0062810) SKUs and has no dedicated spec column in 0K5801 (which jumps 11 to 16 kW) or 0J9943 - treat it as a GT-990 mid-range variant and confirm against a spec sheet.",
   "G0060541 (Honeywell 12 kW) is grouped here by controller generation; neither 0K5801 nor 0J9943 carries a 12 kW spec column, so its true figures sit between the Nexus-era 12 kW numbers and the 13 kW Evolution numbers.",
   "Fuel plenum inspection at every scheduled maintenance - 13 kW and larger air-cooled units built 2008-2015 that have a fuel plenum (the box behind the regulator; Generac dropped it in 2015). A corroded plenum can leak gas while the unit is starting and cause a fire. Unit OFF, front panel and controller cover off, battery terminal covers on (battery can stay in). Feel the plenum: rough, flaking or bubbled = replace it. Gas test: take wire 14 off the fuel solenoid, run a remote switch from the open solenoid terminal to battery +, hold a gas detector by the regulator and plenum and close the switch (gas fills the plenum up to the regulator's secondary shutoff) - any gas = pull the fuel assembly and find the leak. An endoscope down the rear corners of the battery compartment and above the battery shows the plenum surface and welded seam without teardown - any corrosion = replace. Plenums: 0G7581 (1/2 in inlet, before model year 2013), 0J8312 (3/4 in, 20/22 kW 2013 and later), 0J9045 (1/2 in, 2013 and later under 20 kW) - carry the right one (Generac SIB17-01-AAC Rev E, dealer bulletin).",
   "Plenum corrosion is a site problem that builds over time: debris in or around the plenum and regulator, sprinklers hitting the unit, a downspout closer than 36 in, an eave or overhang with no gutter, plants closer than 18 in behind or 36 in at the sides, a bad or damaged pad, or poor drainage (low spot, erosion, standing water). Tell the homeowner so it gets fixed (Generac SIB17-01-AAC Rev E, dealer bulletin).",
   "Hunting (surging) at idle with no load on 16/20/22 kW Evolution units with the 990/999 engine is within design if it stays 58-62 Hz - more noticeable in heat and humidity, when the throttle sits right at the small/large bore transition. With low-speed exercise on it runs slower and won't hunt during exercise, and in an outage it only runs fast with no load for a few seconds at warm-up and cool-down. Running with the lid open or a side panel off, or a dirty air filter, also makes it hunt. Never drill holes or break baffles out of the air box, and never fit an aftermarket air filter (Generac SIB17-20-AAC Rev A, dealer bulletin).",
   "Hunting outside 58-62 Hz, or hunting under load, needs diagnosis: fuel pin set for the fuel in use (NG/LP); fuel pressure static, cranking and running - no more than 1 in. H2O drop from no load to full load and never above the owner's manual maximum; correct supply pipe size and the flex line as straight as possible; throttle linkage not binding; no kinked internal fuel line from regulator to mixer; Test 12 - stepper motor cycles on start; Test 60 - magnetos and the wire 18 signal to the controller; run on one cylinder at a time and note the difference; check valve lash (Generac SIB17-20-AAC Rev A, dealer bulletin)."
  ],
